From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RISE Project CI <ci@riseproject.dev>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CI: trim riscv64's dev-group install and drop its submodule
 re-init

pyproject.toml's dev dependency group (installed wholesale by
1_prerequisites.sh) pulls in pyright, which ships no riscv64 wheel and
has no sdist to fall back to (it wraps a prebuilt Node binary per
platform) -- `pip install --group dev` has no partial-success mode, so
one unresolvable package fails the whole install. On riscv64, install
only what build.py's `pip wheel --no-build-isolation` actually needs:
build-system.requires (scikit-build-core, nanobind, numpy,
setuptools_scm), the cmake/ninja it drives them with, and
psutil/requests (qd_build's own non-stdlib imports). Install ccache
too: setup_sccache() (patched earlier) no-ops on riscv64, and
CMakeLists.txt's own find_program(ccache) is what caches the
per-interpreter rebuilds instead.

The hardcoded `git config --global --add safe.directory
/__w/quadrants/quadrants` assumes upstream's own checkout path; our
workflow checks quadrants out under python-wheels' own job workspace,
not /__w/quadrants/quadrants, and actions/checkout already ran with
submodules: recursive, so the follow-up `git submodule update` is
both redundant and pointed at a path that doesn't exist here. Skip
both on riscv64.

cmake is pinned to the exact 3.31.6 release we publish a riscv64
wheel for (CLAUDE.md gotcha 30). With PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL set, pip
resolves the highest version satisfying a constraint across *both*
indexes and treats a sdist-only candidate as resolvable -- PyPI's
cmake ships no riscv64 wheel for any release, so an unpinned
"cmake<4.0.0" lets pip pick a newer PyPI-only sdist (3.31.10 as of
this port) over our wheel and build CMake itself from source. That
source build FATAL_ERRORs configuring its bundled cmcurl ("Could NOT
find OpenSSL ... Could not find OpenSSL. Install an OpenSSL
development package"), since the manylinux_riscv64 image carries no
OpenSSL dev headers.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64 has no pyright wheel, our workflow checks the repo out at a different path than upstream's own CI does, and PyPI's cmake ships no riscv64 wheel for any release]

Signed-off-by: RISE Project CI <ci@riseproject.dev>
---
 .../manylinux_wheel/1_prerequisites.sh        | 27 +++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/.github/workflows/scripts_new/manylinux_wheel/1_prerequisites.sh b/.github/workflows/scripts_new/manylinux_wheel/1_prerequisites.sh
index 03ca845..eea10bb 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/scripts_new/manylinux_wheel/1_prerequisites.sh
+++ b/.github/workflows/scripts_new/manylinux_wheel/1_prerequisites.sh
@@ -3,12 +3,31 @@
 set -ex

 pip install -U pip
-pip install --group dev
+if [[ "$(uname -m)" == "riscv64" ]]; then
+    # --group dev also pulls pyright, which ships no riscv64 wheel and no sdist to fall back
+    # to (it wraps a prebuilt Node binary); install only what `pip wheel --no-build-isolation`
+    # below actually needs: build.py's own [build-system].requires, plus the cmake/ninja it
+    # drives them with, plus psutil/requests (qd_build's own non-stdlib imports).
+    # Pinned to the exact version we publish a riscv64 wheel for: with PIP_EXTRA_INDEX_URL
+    # pip resolves the highest version across *both* indexes, and PyPI's cmake has no
+    # riscv64 wheel for any release (not even the one we mirror) -- an unpinned "cmake<4.0.0"
+    # lets pip land on a newer PyPI-only sdist (3.31.10 at the time of writing) and build
+    # CMake itself from source, which FATAL_ERRORs configuring its bundled cmcurl because
+    # this image carries no OpenSSL development headers.
+    pip install "cmake==3.31.6" ninja "nanobind>=2.0.0,<2.14.0" "scikit-build-core>=0.10" \
+        "setuptools_scm>=6.0" "numpy>=2.0.0" psutil requests
+    yum install -y ccache  # setup_sccache() no-ops on riscv64; CMakeLists.txt's own
+                           # find_program(ccache) picks this up as the compiler launcher instead.
+else
+    pip install --group dev
+fi

 yum install -y git wget
-# Note: following depends on the name of the repo:
-git config --global --add safe.directory /__w/quadrants/quadrants
-git submodule update --init --jobs 2
+if [[ "$(uname -m)" != "riscv64" ]]; then
+    # Note: following depends on the name of the repo:
+    git config --global --add safe.directory /__w/quadrants/quadrants
+    git submodule update --init --jobs 2
+fi

 LLVM_DIR=$(python download_llvm.py | tail -n 1)
 export PATH=${LLVM_DIR}/bin:$PATH
